From 7b0f82c57081b575692cd2c30449b3e20fc5742d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 14:23:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Support albumentations >= 1.4.15 via a compatibility layer

albumentations 1.4.15 reworked the transform API: the per-item apply_to_bbox()
and apply_to_keypoint() hooks were replaced by batched apply_to_bboxes() /
apply_to_keypoints() taking NumPy arrays, `targets_as_params` together with
get_params_dependent_on_targets() became get_params_dependent_on_data(), the
public keypoint_scale() helper and ElasticTransform's alpha_affine argument were
removed, PadIfNeeded.PositionType became a plain string, `always_apply` was
dropped, and ToGray/CoarseDropout were re-signatured. DeepLabCut's transforms
are written against the older API, so on anything newer they raise -- loudly,
but they raise.

Rather than pinning below the break, adapt the newer releases back to the old
behaviour, gated on albumentations.__version__:

* _keypoints_to_tuples() / _keypoints_from_tuples() / _bboxes_from_sequence()
  translate between the legacy list-of-tuples payloads the transforms are
  written for and the arrays newer albumentations passes.
* KeepAspectRatioResize keeps its per-item hooks and gains batched ones, and
  provides both get_params_dependent_on_targets() and
  get_params_dependent_on_data(); keypoint_scale() becomes keypoints_scale().
* KeypointAwareCrop translates its (h_start, w_start) sampling into the
  crop_coords newer RandomCrop expects.
* ElasticTransform reuses the displacement field newer albumentations already
  sampled for the image (params["map_x"]/["map_y"]) to warp its keypoint
  heatmaps, instead of re-deriving one through the removed
  functional.elastic_transform(). This is strictly better: the keypoints are
  now guaranteed to follow the same field as the image.
* HFlip, CoarseDropout, Grayscale, ScaleToUnitRange and RandomBBoxTransform get
  signature and payload-type adaptations.
* PadIfNeeded is subclassed so that reflecting padding no longer mirrors
  bounding boxes and keypoints into the padded region. Newer albumentations
  does that by design, which turns one annotated instance into several; older
  versions only translated them, and the data pipeline expects the latter.

Verified by running the whole augmentation catalogue on albumentations 1.4.1
and 1.4.20 and diffing: on 1.4.1 the layer is a no-op (byte-identical to
before), and on 1.4.20 every deterministic augmentation now matches 1.4.1
exactly. The four stochastic ones (affine, coarse dropout, elastic, gaussian
noise) differ only in the random draw, as their RNG streams changed upstream;
elastic keypoints were separately checked to track the warped image to
sub-pixel accuracy on both versions.

The cap is widened to `<2` rather than removed: albumentations 2.0 renames more
parameters again (GaussNoise var_limit, the fill/value arguments), which this
layer does not yet cover and which cannot currently be tested because
albumentations 2.x does not import on conda-forge at all.
---
